codemap 9
tour feature-element-tip
kind flow
author ai
group features/gui
note The human, reading in the window, at the moment the pointer reaches a button drawn as a glyph or a word whose effect is not on its face: the panel header's + (pick a view), split right, split down and x, a tab's x, the top bar's back and forward arrows and the settings gear, the tour header's pencil, a step's collapse arrow, save, and a step row's delete; and the Source view's code area, whose select gesture is on the face of nothing and whose toolbar has no room for a sentence saying it. Goal: understanding a codebase takes less friction through codemap than through an editor; a reader who stops to decode the window's own controls is not reading the code. Seen in runs at 1600x1000 before this feature: resting on pick, split-right, split-down, close-panel and save showed nothing (owner's screenshots), and the parent build playing the gui-document scenario printed tip=None after resting 600 ms on a step's delete, on split-right/1 and on save; no code path drew anything for a button under the pointer, so the other glyphs listed were the same. To learn what split-down or x does the reader clicks it and then undoes the split or brings the view back; clicking a step's delete changes the map. Save's ctrl+s and back's alt+left were written only on the start page, which is gone once a tour is open, and in the palette, which shows them only to someone who opens it and types the action's name: neither is where the pointer is when the question arises. A step row's delete moves the step's children up to its parent, while unticking a step in the edit form removes its subtree, and nothing on screen told the two apart; the owner keeps both behaviours and wants the difference visible, and a longer label on every step row would crowd a header that already holds hide code, whole symbol and link buttons, while a tip costs no room until asked for. Nothing codemap had answers it: the hover card (feature-hover-info) explains identifiers in code only; the palette and the start page key list are elsewhere, as above; the registry already held a one-line summary per feature, defined as the button's tooltip, but nothing in the window showed it. The design (one, by the owner's decision; no rival prototypes): every Target carries the Feature of the Control it was made from, so a view attaches a tip with frame.attach_tip(target) after drawing the element, and the text is that feature's registry summary followed by every key chord bound to it, the same summary the CLI help and palette show, so each concept keeps one description. The tip shows after the pointer rests on one element for 500 ms, sits just below the element (above it when the window has no room below) and is moved sideways to stay inside the window, so it never covers what it names; one tip at a time, the code hover card winning when both are wanted; a press hides it until the pointer moves to another element. A run: resting on split-right/1 shows 'split a panel in two, to the right or below' below the button; on save, 'write the map's changed tours to disk  ctrl+s'; on a step's delete, 'delete a step; its children move up to its parent'; pressing save hides the tip and it stays hidden while the pointer stays. dump prints the shown tip as tip=, and the box is the element tip, so rect element-tip gives its place.

